I measured the claim and it holds: after /cd, the launch project's MCP servers stay connected. That's now written in the docs as a deliberate decision. I committed it as 912057d0, which hasn't been pushed yet; the push will close #869.

How I measured it: I ran the built binary through a pty against a stub OpenAI-compatible server. Launch dir A had a small stdio MCP server that writes its PID to a file and offers one tool, probe869_tool, and I started with --trust-project.
• The server process (PID 58879) was still alive after /cd ../B, where trust switched OFF, and again after /cd ../A.
• /mcp showed "1 connected" every time.
• The tool list sent with each prompt, in A and then in B, still included probe869_tool.
• I didn't check whether the model can actually call the tool after /cd. I've said so in the #869 comment.

What changed:
• `docs/src/configuration/permissions.md`: the old sentence came from reading the code, not from a run. I replaced it with a paragraph giving the decision and its reason: you approved these servers at startup, their tools are already in the tool list, and dropping one mid-session would mean rebuilding the agent, which drops them all (#842). It also explains why this differs from the launch project's allow patterns and hooks, which pause outside its tree, and points to #1040 for a new directory's own servers.
• `src/cd_config_note.rs`: closing #869 would have left the /cd note telling users "Restart yoyo here to use them (#869)", pointing at a closed ticket. I changed 4 user-facing strings and their 12 test expectations to cite #1040, which I checked is open and covers that gap.
• New issue #1042: a check-path register in the tests listed the instruction-file loader with the reason "runs at startup, before any /cd". That's false for /spawn: src/commands_spawn.rs:510 reloads instruction files from the current directory when you run it, and only checks the startup [directories] fence. I found this by reading the code and haven't reproduced it in a run. I filed it so closing #869 doesn't leave it untracked, and named it in the register and the docs. The test that requires each reason to mention "#869" is unchanged.

Build, clippy, fmt and cargo test (6640 passed, 0 failed) all pass. I've commented on #869 with the raw output and a status for each item.
